"use strict";(self.webpackChunkenterprise_ai_agents_applied_ml_book=self.webpackChunkenterprise_ai_agents_applied_ml_book||[]).push([[3019],{9088(e,t,n){n.r(t),n.d(t,{assets:()=>c,contentTitle:()=>l,default:()=>m,frontMatter:()=>d,metadata:()=>a,toc:()=>h});const a=JSON.parse('{"id":"part5-deep-learning-and-llms/transformers-and-attention","title":"Transformers & Attention \u2014 From RNN Memory to GPT Generation","description":"Read this chapter as one continuous design path. We are not introducing attention as a separate trick. We are answering the question left open by GRU/LSTM: if a decoder needs a specific old token, why force that infor...","source":"@site/docs/part5-deep-learning-and-llms/11-transformers-and-attention.mdx","sourceDirName":"part5-deep-learning-and-llms","slug":"/part5-deep-learning-and-llms/11-transformers-and-attention","permalink":"/tutorials/part5-deep-learning-and-llms/11-transformers-and-attention","draft":false,"unlisted":false,"tags":[],"version":"current","lastUpdatedAt":1785375762000,"sidebarPosition":11,"frontMatter":{"title":"Transformers & Attention \u2014 From RNN Memory to GPT Generation","slug":"/part5-deep-learning-and-llms/11-transformers-and-attention","description":"Read this chapter as one continuous design path. We are not introducing attention as a separate trick. We are answering the question left open by GRU/LSTM: if a decoder needs a specific old token, why force that infor...","sidebar_label":"11. Transformers and attention","pagination_label":"11. Transformers and attention","hide_title":true,"hide_table_of_contents":true},"sidebar":"bookSidebar","previous":{"title":"10. Recurrent neural networks","permalink":"/tutorials/part5-deep-learning-and-llms/10-recurrent-neural-networks"},"next":{"title":"12. LLM fine-tuning","permalink":"/tutorials/part5-deep-learning-and-llms/12-llm-fine-tuning"}}');var o=n(4848),r=n(8453),i=n(8172);const s=JSON.parse('{"sourcePath":"part5-deep-learning-and-llms/11-transformers-and-attention.html","route":"/part5-deep-learning-and-llms/11-transformers-and-attention","kind":"chapter","partId":"part5","partLabel":"Part V - Deep Learning & Large Language Models","accent":"coral","heading":"Transformers & Attention: from RNN memory to GPT generation","title":"Transformers & Attention \u2014 From RNN Memory to GPT Generation","description":"Read this chapter as one continuous design path. We are not introducing attention as a separate trick. We are answering the question left open by GRU/LSTM: if a decoder needs a specific old token, why force that infor...","readingMinutes":15,"bodyClass":"","normalizeMath":false,"toc":[{"id":"1-start-from-the-rnn-problem-one-vector-is-too-small-a-bridge","label":"1) Start from the RNN problem: one vector is too small a bridge"},{"id":"2-cross-attention-the-decoder-asks-the-source-sentence-a-question","label":"2) Cross-attention: the decoder asks the source sentence a question"},{"id":"3-self-attention-make-every-token-both-reader-and-memory","label":"3) Self-attention: make every token both reader and memory"},{"id":"4-causal-self-attention-the-gpt-training-and-generation-mental-model","label":"4) Causal self-attention: the GPT training and generation mental model"},{"id":"5-the-intuition-a-soft-content-based-dictionary-lookup","label":"5) The intuition: a soft, content-based dictionary lookup"},{"id":"6-scaled-dot-product-attention","label":"6) Scaled dot-product attention"},{"id":"7-multi-head-attention","label":"7) Multi-head attention"},{"id":"8-position-information-sinusoidal-vs-rope","label":"8) Position information: sinusoidal vs RoPE"},{"id":"9-the-full-transformer-block","label":"9) The full Transformer block"},{"id":"10-encoder-decoder-encoder-decoder","label":"10) Encoder, decoder, encoder-decoder"},{"id":"11-inference-kv-cache","label":"11) Inference: KV cache"},{"id":"12-efficiency-flashattention-mqa-gqa-sliding-window","label":"12) Efficiency: FlashAttention, MQA, GQA, sliding window"},{"id":"13-pulling-it-all-together","label":"13) Pulling it all together"}],"html":"<div class=\\"ch\\">\\n\\n\\n\\n<div class=\\"tr-note\\">\\n  <strong>Where this fits</strong>\\n  Chapter 10 gave you encoder-decoder RNNs: the encoder reads a sequence,\\n  the decoder generates a sequence, and hidden state carries information\\n  forward. This chapter keeps that mental model but removes the fragile\\n  bottleneck. First the decoder learns to <em>look back</em> at encoder states\\n  using cross-attention. Then tokens learn to look at other tokens in the same\\n  sequence using self-attention. Finally, causal self-attention gives us\\n  GPT-style next-token generation.\\n</div>\\n\\n<p>\\n  Read this chapter as one continuous design path. We are not introducing attention as a separate trick. We are answering\\n  the question left open by GRU/LSTM: if a decoder needs a specific old token, why force that information to survive only\\n  through recurrent memory? Cross-attention lets a decoder look up source states directly. Self-attention then applies the\\n  same lookup idea inside a single sequence. Causal self-attention finally makes that lookup safe for next-token generation.\\n</p>\\n\\n<h2 id=\\"1-start-from-the-rnn-problem-one-vector-is-too-small-a-bridge\\">1) Start from the RNN problem: one vector is too small a bridge</h2>\\n\\n<p>\\n  In the RNN translation chapter, the encoder read <code>my name is dinesh</code>\\n  and passed a final hidden state to the decoder. That works as a first mental\\n  model, but it has a bottleneck: the decoder must recover every useful source\\n  detail from one vector. For a short sentence this is manageable. For a long\\n  paragraph, one compressed state is a weak memory interface.\\n</p>\\n\\n<p>\\n  LSTMs reduce the damage because their cell state can preserve information better than a vanilla RNN or a GRU. But even\\n  an LSTM encoder-decoder still asks the decoder to recover source details through recurrent state. The next idea is to keep\\n  every encoder state available and let the decoder choose which ones matter at each output step.\\n</p>\\n\\n<div class=\\"grid\\">\\n  <div class=\\"card\\">\\n    <h3 id=\\"rnn-seq2seq-bottleneck\\">RNN seq2seq bottleneck</h3>\\n    <ul>\\n      <li>Encoder reads source tokens one by one.</li>\\n      <li>Final hidden state summarizes the whole source.</li>\\n      <li>Decoder depends heavily on that single summary.</li>\\n      <li>Long-range details can fade through time.</li>\\n    </ul>\\n  </div>\\n  <div class=\\"card\\">\\n    <h3 id=\\"attention-idea\\">Attention idea</h3>\\n    <ul>\\n      <li>Keep the encoder state at every source position.</li>\\n      <li>At each decoder step, ask: \u201cwhich source tokens matter now?\u201d</li>\\n      <li>Take a weighted average of the relevant source states.</li>\\n      <li>The decoder gets a fresh context vector at every output step.</li>\\n    </ul>\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <svg class=\\"viz\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 980 390\\" role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"RNN bottleneck compared with attention lookup\\">\\n    <defs>\\n      <marker id=\\"attn-arrow-blue\\" markerWidth=\\"10\\" markerHeight=\\"10\\" refX=\\"8\\" refY=\\"3\\" orient=\\"auto\\">\\n        <path d=\\"M0,0 L8,3 L0,6 Z\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\"></path>\\n      </marker>\\n      <marker id=\\"attn-arrow-purple\\" markerWidth=\\"10\\" markerHeight=\\"10\\" refX=\\"8\\" refY=\\"3\\" orient=\\"auto\\">\\n        <path d=\\"M0,0 L8,3 L0,6 Z\\" fill=\\"#8250df\\"></path>\\n      </marker>\\n    </defs>\\n    <text x=\\"28\\" y=\\"34\\" class=\\"title\\">From RNN bottleneck to attention</text>\\n    <text x=\\"28\\" y=\\"60\\" class=\\"muted\\">Attention does not force the decoder to depend on one final encoder vector.</text>\\n\\n    <text x=\\"165\\" y=\\"96\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">RNN encoder-decoder</text>\\n    <rect x=\\"50\\" y=\\"125\\" width=\\"76\\" height=\\"42\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n    <rect x=\\"140\\" y=\\"125\\" width=\\"76\\" height=\\"42\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n    <rect x=\\"230\\" y=\\"125\\" width=\\"76\\" height=\\"42\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"88\\" y=\\"151\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">my</text>\\n    <text x=\\"178\\" y=\\"151\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">name</text>\\n    <text x=\\"268\\" y=\\"151\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">...</text>\\n    <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M306 146 L372 146\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\" stroke-width=\\"2.4\\" marker-end=\\"url(#attn-arrow-blue)\\"></path>\\n    <rect x=\\"378\\" y=\\"110\\" width=\\"126\\" height=\\"72\\" rx=\\"12\\" fill=\\"#f5f0ff\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"441\\" y=\\"140\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">one final</text>\\n    <text x=\\"441\\" y=\\"162\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">hidden vector</text>\\n    <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M504 146 L580 146\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\" stroke-width=\\"2.4\\" marker-end=\\"url(#attn-arrow-purple)\\"></path>\\n    <rect x=\\"586\\" y=\\"125\\" width=\\"98\\" height=\\"42\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#f3f8f1\\" stroke=\\"#1a7f37\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"635\\" y=\\"151\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">decoder</text>\\n\\n    <text x=\\"175\\" y=\\"238\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">Attention bridge</text>\\n    <rect x=\\"50\\" y=\\"268\\" width=\\"76\\" height=\\"42\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n    <rect x=\\"140\\" y=\\"268\\" width=\\"76\\" height=\\"42\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n    <rect x=\\"230\\" y=\\"268\\" width=\\"76\\" height=\\"42\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n    <rect x=\\"320\\" y=\\"268\\" width=\\"76\\" height=\\"42\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"88\\" y=\\"294\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">h1</text>\\n    <text x=\\"178\\" y=\\"294\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">h2</text>\\n    <text x=\\"268\\" y=\\"294\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">h3</text>\\n    <text x=\\"358\\" y=\\"294\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">h4</text>\\n    <rect x=\\"586\\" y=\\"268\\" width=\\"98\\" height=\\"42\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#f3f8f1\\" stroke=\\"#1a7f37\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"635\\" y=\\"294\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">decoder</text>\\n    <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M590 268 C510 218 360 232 91 268\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\" stroke-width=\\"2.2\\" marker-end=\\"url(#attn-arrow-purple)\\"></path>\\n    <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M599 268 C520 235 410 245 180 268\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\" stroke-width=\\"2.2\\" marker-end=\\"url(#attn-arrow-purple)\\"></path>\\n    <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M622 267 C565 245 500 252 358 268\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\" stroke-width=\\"2.2\\" marker-end=\\"url(#attn-arrow-purple)\\"></path>\\n    <text x=\\"535\\" y=\\"236\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">decoder query looks back at all encoder states</text>\\n  </svg>\\n  <figcaption>\\n    The key step from RNNs to attention is simple: keep all encoder states, then let the decoder choose which ones to use.\\n  </figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n<h2 id=\\"2-cross-attention-the-decoder-asks-the-source-sentence-a-question\\">2) Cross-attention: the decoder asks the source sentence a question</h2>\\n\\n<p>\\n  Cross-attention is the easiest form to understand after RNN translation.\\n  The decoder is currently trying to produce a target token. It builds a\\n  <strong>query</strong>: \u201cwhat source information do I need right now?\u201d The\\n  encoder states provide <strong>keys</strong>: \u201cwhat information can I match\\n  against?\u201d and <strong>values</strong>: \u201cwhat information should I return if\\n  matched?\u201d\\n</p>\\n\\n<div class=\\"eq\\">\\n  \\\\[\\n    Q = H_{\\\\text{decoder}} W_Q,\\\\quad\\n    K = H_{\\\\text{encoder}} W_K,\\\\quad\\n    V = H_{\\\\text{encoder}} W_V\\n  \\\\]\\n  \\\\[\\n    \\\\text{CrossAttention}(Q,K,V)\\n    = \\\\text{softmax}\\\\!\\\\left(\\\\frac{QK^\\\\top}{\\\\sqrt{d_k}}\\\\right)V\\n  \\\\]\\n</div>\\n\\n<table>\\n  <tbody><tr><th>Object</th><th>Comes from</th><th>Meaning in translation</th></tr>\\n  <tr>\\n    <td>Query \\\\(Q\\\\)</td>\\n    <td>Decoder hidden state</td>\\n    <td>The output side asking what source context it needs for this target step.</td>\\n  </tr>\\n  <tr>\\n    <td>Keys \\\\(K\\\\)</td>\\n    <td>Encoder states</td>\\n    <td>Search handles for each source token/state.</td>\\n  </tr>\\n  <tr>\\n    <td>Values \\\\(V\\\\)</td>\\n    <td>Encoder states</td>\\n    <td>The information blended into the decoder context vector.</td>\\n  </tr>\\n</tbody></table>\\n\\n<p>\\n  For the example <code>my name is dinesh</code> \u2192 <code>mon nom est dinesh</code>,\\n  when the decoder is about to produce <code>dinesh</code>, its query should\\n  strongly match the encoder state for the source name token. The attention\\n  weights are learned, soft alignments; they are not hand-coded word links.\\n</p>\\n\\n<div class=\\"tr-tip\\">\\n  <strong>What cross-attention fixes</strong>\\n  The decoder no longer depends only on the final encoder hidden state. Each output step gets a fresh context vector\\n  computed from all source positions. This is the direct answer to the seq2seq bottleneck.\\n</div>\\n\\n<p>\\n  Cross-attention still assumes two sides: a source memory and a decoder query. That is perfect for translation, but it\\n  raises the next question: if attention is just content-based lookup, do we need a recurrent encoder at all? Could each\\n  token in a sentence look directly at the other tokens and build its representation in parallel?\\n</p>\\n\\n<h2 id=\\"3-self-attention-make-every-token-both-reader-and-memory\\">3) Self-attention: make every token both reader and memory</h2>\\n\\n<p>\\n  Cross-attention has two sides: decoder queries, encoder keys/values.\\n  Self-attention removes that separation. The same sequence supplies queries,\\n  keys, and values:\\n</p>\\n\\n<div class=\\"eq\\">\\n  \\\\[\\n    Q = XW_Q,\\\\quad K = XW_K,\\\\quad V = XW_V\\n  \\\\]\\n</div>\\n\\n<p>\\n  Now each token can ask every token in the same sequence for context. In an\\n  encoder, this is usually bidirectional: every token can look left and right.\\n  In a decoder-only language model, this must be causal: each position can\\n  look only at itself and earlier positions.\\n</p>\\n\\n<p>\\n  This is the move from \u201cdecoder looks at encoder memory\u201d to \u201ctokens are the memory.\u201d Self-attention removes the recurrent\\n  scan inside the encoder because all token-to-token relationships can be scored at once. That is why Transformers train\\n  so much more efficiently than RNNs on modern hardware. The price is that attention by itself has no sense of order and,\\n  for generation, no built-in protection against peeking at future tokens. Those two pressures lead to positional encoding\\n  and causal masking.\\n</p>\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <svg class=\\"viz\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 980 430\\" role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"Cross attention self attention and causal self attention patterns\\">\\n    <text x=\\"28\\" y=\\"34\\" class=\\"title\\">Attention patterns: cross, self, causal self</text>\\n    <text x=\\"28\\" y=\\"60\\" class=\\"muted\\">The same Q/K/V operation changes behavior depending on where Q, K, V come from and which positions are masked.</text>\\n\\n    <g transform=\\"translate(35,105)\\">\\n      <text x=\\"130\\" y=\\"-18\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">Cross-attention</text>\\n      <text x=\\"130\\" y=\\"7\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">decoder queries source memory</text>\\n      <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"32\\" width=\\"260\\" height=\\"48\\" rx=\\"10\\" fill=\\"#f3f8f1\\" stroke=\\"#1a7f37\\"></rect>\\n      <text x=\\"130\\" y=\\"62\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">decoder query: \u201cwhat do I need?\u201d</text>\\n      <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"130\\" width=\\"72\\" height=\\"46\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n      <rect x=\\"94\\" y=\\"130\\" width=\\"72\\" height=\\"46\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n      <rect x=\\"188\\" y=\\"130\\" width=\\"72\\" height=\\"46\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n      <text x=\\"36\\" y=\\"158\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">src 1</text>\\n      <text x=\\"130\\" y=\\"158\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">src 2</text>\\n      <text x=\\"224\\" y=\\"158\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">src 3</text>\\n      <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M130 80 L36 130\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"></path>\\n      <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M130 80 L130 130\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"></path>\\n      <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M130 80 L224 130\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\"></path>\\n    </g>\\n\\n    <g transform=\\"translate(365,105)\\">\\n      <text x=\\"130\\" y=\\"-18\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">Self-attention</text>\\n      <text x=\\"130\\" y=\\"7\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">tokens read the same sequence</text>\\n      <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"88\\" width=\\"64\\" height=\\"46\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#f5f0ff\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\"></rect>\\n      <rect x=\\"98\\" y=\\"88\\" width=\\"64\\" height=\\"46\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#f5f0ff\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\"></rect>\\n      <rect x=\\"196\\" y=\\"88\\" width=\\"64\\" height=\\"46\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#f5f0ff\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\"></rect>\\n      <text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"116\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">tok 1</text>\\n      <text x=\\"130\\" y=\\"116\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">tok 2</text>\\n      <text x=\\"228\\" y=\\"116\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">tok 3</text>\\n      <path d=\\"M32 88 C45 35 115 35 130 88\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\" fill=\\"none\\"></path>\\n      <path d=\\"M130 88 C145 35 215 35 228 88\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\" fill=\\"none\\"></path>\\n      <path d=\\"M228 134 C195 195 65 195 32 134\\" stroke=\\"#1a7f37\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\" fill=\\"none\\"></path>\\n      <text x=\\"130\\" y=\\"225\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">no mask: each token may use all tokens</text>\\n    </g>\\n\\n    <g transform=\\"translate(695,105)\\">\\n      <text x=\\"130\\" y=\\"-18\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">Causal self-attention</text>\\n      <text x=\\"130\\" y=\\"7\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">future positions are hidden</text>\\n      <g transform=\\"translate(42,40)\\">\\n        <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"36\\" height=\\"36\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".85\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"42\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"36\\" height=\\"36\\" fill=\\"#f6f8fa\\" stroke=\\"#d0d7de\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"84\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"36\\" height=\\"36\\" fill=\\"#f6f8fa\\" stroke=\\"#d0d7de\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"126\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"36\\" height=\\"36\\" fill=\\"#f6f8fa\\" stroke=\\"#d0d7de\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"42\\" width=\\"36\\" height=\\"36\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".65\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"42\\" y=\\"42\\" width=\\"36\\" height=\\"36\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".85\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"84\\" y=\\"42\\" width=\\"36\\" height=\\"36\\" fill=\\"#f6f8fa\\" stroke=\\"#d0d7de\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"126\\" y=\\"42\\" width=\\"36\\" height=\\"36\\" fill=\\"#f6f8fa\\" stroke=\\"#d0d7de\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"84\\" width=\\"36\\" height=\\"36\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".45\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"42\\" y=\\"84\\" width=\\"36\\" height=\\"36\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".65\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"84\\" y=\\"84\\" width=\\"36\\" height=\\"36\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".85\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"126\\" y=\\"84\\" width=\\"36\\" height=\\"36\\" fill=\\"#f6f8fa\\" stroke=\\"#d0d7de\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"126\\" width=\\"36\\" height=\\"36\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".35\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"42\\" y=\\"126\\" width=\\"36\\" height=\\"36\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".45\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"84\\" y=\\"126\\" width=\\"36\\" height=\\"36\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".65\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"126\\" y=\\"126\\" width=\\"36\\" height=\\"36\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".85\\"></rect>\\n      </g>\\n      <text x=\\"122\\" y=\\"226\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">lower triangle = visible context</text>\\n    </g>\\n  </svg>\\n  <figcaption>\\n    Cross-attention reads a separate memory. Self-attention reads the same sequence. Causal self-attention blocks the future.\\n  </figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n<h2 id=\\"4-causal-self-attention-the-gpt-training-and-generation-mental-model\\">4) Causal self-attention: the GPT training and generation mental model</h2>\\n\\n<p>\\n  If self-attention lets every token look at every other token, a language generator needs one more rule: the future must\\n  be invisible. GPT is a decoder-only Transformer: a stack of masked self-attention blocks plus feed-forward networks. It\\n  is trained to predict the next token from the previous tokens. The important correction is that position \\\\(t\\\\) can attend\\n  to positions \\\\(\\\\le t\\\\), and its hidden state is used to predict token \\\\(t+1\\\\). It does not peek at future target tokens.\\n</p>\\n\\n<table>\\n  <tbody><tr><th>Prefix available at a position</th><th>Training target</th></tr>\\n  <tr><td><code>my</code></td><td><code>name</code></td></tr>\\n  <tr><td><code>my name</code></td><td><code>is</code></td></tr>\\n  <tr><td><code>my name is</code></td><td><code>Dinesh</code></td></tr>\\n  <tr><td><code>my name is Dinesh</code></td><td><code>&lt;EOS&gt;</code> or next punctuation/token</td></tr>\\n</tbody></table>\\n\\n<div class=\\"eq\\">\\n  \\\\[\\n    p(x_{t+1}\\\\mid x_1,\\\\dots,x_t)\\n    = \\\\text{softmax}(W_{\\\\text{vocab}} h_t)\\n  \\\\]\\n</div>\\n\\n<p>\\n  During training, the full sequence is processed in parallel with a causal\\n  mask. During inference, generation is autoregressive: predict one token,\\n  append it, then predict the next token from the extended prefix.\\n</p>\\n\\n<table>\\n  <tbody><tr><th>Phase</th><th>What the model receives</th><th>What it predicts</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>\\n  <tr>\\n    <td>Training</td>\\n    <td>A full token sequence, processed in parallel under a causal mask.</td>\\n    <td>The next token for every visible prefix position.</td>\\n    <td>Efficient learning: one forward pass creates many next-token training examples.</td>\\n  </tr>\\n  <tr>\\n    <td>Inference step 1</td>\\n    <td><code>[my, name, is]</code></td>\\n    <td>A distribution where <code>Dinesh</code> may be the selected token.</td>\\n    <td>The model emits one token, not a whole final sentence in one atomic action.</td>\\n  </tr>\\n  <tr>\\n    <td>Inference step 2</td>\\n    <td><code>[my, name, is, Dinesh]</code></td>\\n    <td>The next distribution, perhaps selecting <code>.</code>.</td>\\n    <td>The generated token becomes part of the next input prefix.</td>\\n  </tr>\\n  <tr>\\n    <td>Stopping</td>\\n    <td><code>[my, name, is, Dinesh, .]</code></td>\\n    <td><code>&lt;EOS&gt;</code>, another token, or a stop caused by length limits.</td>\\n    <td>Generation repeats until a stop condition is met.</td>\\n  </tr>\\n  <tr>\\n    <td>KV caching</td>\\n    <td>The new token plus cached keys/values from earlier positions.</td>\\n    <td>The next-token distribution without recomputing all old attention states.</td>\\n    <td>Same conceptual loop, much lower decode cost in real systems.</td>\\n  </tr>\\n</tbody></table>\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <svg class=\\"viz\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 980 360\\" role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"GPT autoregressive generation loop\\">\\n    <defs>\\n      <marker id=\\"gpt-loop-arrow\\" markerWidth=\\"10\\" markerHeight=\\"10\\" refX=\\"8\\" refY=\\"3\\" orient=\\"auto\\">\\n        <path d=\\"M0,0 L8,3 L0,6 Z\\" fill=\\"#8250df\\"></path>\\n      </marker>\\n    </defs>\\n    <text x=\\"28\\" y=\\"34\\" class=\\"title\\">GPT generation loop</text>\\n    <text x=\\"28\\" y=\\"60\\" class=\\"muted\\">Training predicts every next token under a mask. Inference appends one sampled token at a time.</text>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"60\\" y=\\"110\\" width=\\"210\\" height=\\"68\\" rx=\\"12\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"165\\" y=\\"138\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">input tokens</text>\\n    <text x=\\"165\\" y=\\"160\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">my name is</text>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"355\\" y=\\"95\\" width=\\"245\\" height=\\"98\\" rx=\\"14\\" fill=\\"#f5f0ff\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\" stroke-width=\\"1.6\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"478\\" y=\\"127\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">decoder-only Transformer</text>\\n    <text x=\\"478\\" y=\\"151\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">masked self-attention</text>\\n    <text x=\\"478\\" y=\\"174\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">logits at last position</text>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"690\\" y=\\"110\\" width=\\"210\\" height=\\"68\\" rx=\\"12\\" fill=\\"#f3f8f1\\" stroke=\\"#1a7f37\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"795\\" y=\\"138\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">next-token distribution</text>\\n    <text x=\\"795\\" y=\\"160\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">Dinesh, ., ...</text>\\n\\n    <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M270 144 L355 144\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\" stroke-width=\\"2.4\\" marker-end=\\"url(#gpt-loop-arrow)\\"></path>\\n    <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M600 144 L690 144\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\" stroke-width=\\"2.4\\" marker-end=\\"url(#gpt-loop-arrow)\\"></path>\\n    <path d=\\"M795 178 C795 282 170 282 170 180\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\" stroke-width=\\"2.6\\" fill=\\"none\\" marker-end=\\"url(#gpt-loop-arrow)\\"></path>\\n    <text x=\\"485\\" y=\\"306\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">sample / choose token, append to prefix, repeat until stop token or length limit</text>\\n  </svg>\\n  <figcaption>\\n    The output token becomes part of the next input prefix. KV caching makes this efficient by reusing previous keys and values.\\n  </figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n<pre><code class=\\"language-python\\"># Conceptual GPT inference loop.\\n# Real systems batch requests, keep per-layer KV caches, and use sampling controls.\\n\\ntokens = tokenize(\\"my name is\\")\\n\\nwhile len(tokens) &lt; max_len:\\n    logits = gpt(tokens)                        # simple mental model: full prefix\\n    next_logits = logits[-1]                    # distribution for the next token\\n    next_token = sample(next_logits, temperature=0.8, top_p=0.95)\\n\\n    tokens.append(next_token)                   # feed output back as input\\n    if next_token == EOS_ID:\\n        break</code></pre>\\n\\n<div class=\\"tr-warn\\">\\n  <strong>Training vs inference</strong>\\n  Training can process all positions at once because the causal mask prevents\\n  future leakage. Inference must generate one new token at a time because the\\n  next input token does not exist until the model has produced it.\\n</div>\\n\\n<p>\\n  Now the architectural flow is complete enough to name the math. Cross-attention, self-attention, and causal self-attention\\n  are not three unrelated mechanisms. They are the same query-key-value operation with different sources for \\\\(Q,K,V\\\\) and\\n  different masks. The next section writes that common operation once.\\n</p>\\n\\n<h2 id=\\"5-the-intuition-a-soft-content-based-dictionary-lookup\\">5) The intuition: a soft, content-based dictionary lookup</h2>\\n\\n<p>Think of attention as a dictionary lookup that is differentiable.</p>\\n<ul>\\n  <li>For each token you want to <em>update</em>, you compute a <strong>query</strong> vector\\n      <em>q</em>: \\"what kind of context do I need?\\"</li>\\n  <li>For every token in the sequence (including itself) you have a\\n      <strong>key</strong> <em>k</em> (\\"what kind of context do I provide?\\") and a\\n      <strong>value</strong> <em>v</em> (\\"what would I contribute if attended to?\\").</li>\\n  <li>You score each (q, k) pair, turn the scores into a probability\\n      distribution with softmax, and return the weighted average of values.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<p>That\'s it. The whole Transformer is this operation, stacked, with a small\\nfeed\u2011forward network in between, and residual connections + LayerNorm.</p>\\n\\n<h2 id=\\"6-scaled-dot-product-attention\\">6) Scaled dot-product attention</h2>\\n\\n<p>Stack the queries, keys and values into matrices $Q \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{n\\\\times d_k}$,\\n$K \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{n\\\\times d_k}$, $V \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{n\\\\times d_v}$ where $n$ is the\\nsequence length. Then:</p>\\n\\n<div class=\\"eq\\">\\n$\\\\mathrm{Attention}(Q,K,V) = \\\\mathrm{softmax}\\\\!\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{QK^{\\\\top}}{\\\\sqrt{d_k}}\\\\right) V$\\n</div>\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <svg class=\\"viz\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 980 300\\" role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"Scaled dot-product attention calculation flow\\">\\n    <defs>\\n      <marker id=\\"scaled-attn-arrow\\" markerWidth=\\"10\\" markerHeight=\\"10\\" refX=\\"8\\" refY=\\"3\\" orient=\\"auto\\">\\n        <path d=\\"M0,0 L8,3 L0,6 Z\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\"></path>\\n      </marker>\\n    </defs>\\n    <text x=\\"30\\" y=\\"34\\" class=\\"title\\">Scaled dot-product attention as a soft lookup</text>\\n    <text x=\\"30\\" y=\\"60\\" class=\\"muted\\">Queries ask questions, keys are compared against those questions, values are blended by the resulting weights.</text>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"58\\" y=\\"126\\" width=\\"104\\" height=\\"70\\" rx=\\"12\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n    <rect x=\\"58\\" y=\\"206\\" width=\\"104\\" height=\\"42\\" rx=\\"10\\" fill=\\"#f3f8f1\\" stroke=\\"#1a7f37\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"110\\" y=\\"154\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">Q</text>\\n    <text x=\\"110\\" y=\\"176\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">queries</text>\\n    <text x=\\"110\\" y=\\"232\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">K</text>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"246\\" y=\\"136\\" width=\\"148\\" height=\\"58\\" rx=\\"12\\" fill=\\"#f5f0ff\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"320\\" y=\\"160\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">scores</text>\\n    <text x=\\"320\\" y=\\"181\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">QK^T</text>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"468\\" y=\\"136\\" width=\\"150\\" height=\\"58\\" rx=\\"12\\" fill=\\"#fff8e6\\" stroke=\\"#bf8700\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"543\\" y=\\"160\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">scale + mask</text>\\n    <text x=\\"543\\" y=\\"181\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">divide by sqrt(d_k)</text>\\n\\n    <g transform=\\"translate(680,112)\\">\\n      <text x=\\"80\\" y=\\"-10\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">softmax weights</text>\\n      <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"20\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".95\\"></rect>\\n      <rect x=\\"40\\" y=\\"20\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".45\\"></rect>\\n      <rect x=\\"80\\" y=\\"20\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".15\\"></rect>\\n      <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"60\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".25\\"></rect>\\n      <rect x=\\"40\\" y=\\"60\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".9\\"></rect>\\n      <rect x=\\"80\\" y=\\"60\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".35\\"></rect>\\n      <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"100\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".2\\"></rect>\\n      <rect x=\\"40\\" y=\\"100\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".35\\"></rect>\\n      <rect x=\\"80\\" y=\\"100\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\" opacity=\\".85\\"></rect>\\n    </g>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"850\\" y=\\"136\\" width=\\"94\\" height=\\"58\\" rx=\\"12\\" fill=\\"#f3f8f1\\" stroke=\\"#1a7f37\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"897\\" y=\\"160\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">weighted</text>\\n    <text x=\\"897\\" y=\\"181\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">values</text>\\n    <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M162 160 L246 160 M394 165 L468 165 M618 165 L680 165 M804 165 L850 165\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\" stroke-width=\\"2.4\\" marker-end=\\"url(#scaled-attn-arrow)\\"></path>\\n    <path d=\\"M162 227 C196 227 202 177 246 174\\" stroke=\\"#1a7f37\\" stroke-width=\\"2.1\\" fill=\\"none\\" marker-end=\\"url(#scaled-attn-arrow)\\"></path>\\n    <text x=\\"520\\" y=\\"248\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">causal masks set future scores to -infinity before softmax</text>\\n  </svg>\\n  <figcaption>\\n    Scaled dot-product attention is a soft lookup: compare queries to keys, mask if needed, normalize into weights, then blend values.\\n  </figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n<p>The $\\\\sqrt{d_k}$ scaling keeps the dot products from growing too large with\\ndimension, which would push softmax into a near\u2011one\u2011hot regime and kill the\\ngradients. Implemented in PyTorch:</p>\\n\\n<pre><code class=\\"language-python\\">import torch, torch.nn.functional as F\\n\\ndef scaled_dot_product_attention(Q, K, V, mask=None):\\n    d_k = Q.size(-1)\\n    scores = (Q @ K.transpose(-2, -1)) / d_k**0.5      # (..., n, n)\\n    if mask is not None:\\n        scores = scores.masked_fill(mask == 0, float(\\"-inf\\"))\\n    weights = F.softmax(scores, dim=-1)                # (..., n, n)\\n    return weights @ V                                 # (..., n, d_v)\\n</code></pre>\\n\\n<h3 id=\\"causal-decoder-masking\\">Causal (decoder) masking</h3>\\n<p>For language modeling, position $i$ may only attend to positions\\n$\\\\le i$. The mask is the lower triangular matrix of ones; everything\\nabove the diagonal is set to $-\\\\infty$ before softmax.</p>\\n\\n<pre><code class=\\"language-python\\">n = Q.size(-2)\\ncausal = torch.tril(torch.ones(n, n, device=Q.device))\\nout = scaled_dot_product_attention(Q, K, V, mask=causal)\\n</code></pre>\\n\\n<p>\\n  One attention operation gives one way to judge relevance. That is already more flexible than recurrent state, but language\\n  has many relationships at once: subject-verb agreement, reference resolution, local syntax, topic, and formatting. This\\n  leads directly to multi-head attention.\\n</p>\\n\\n<h2 id=\\"7-multi-head-attention\\">7) Multi-head attention</h2>\\n\\n<p>One attention head learns one notion of relevance. Real language has many\\nin parallel \u2014 syntactic agreement, coreference, topic, etc. Multi\u2011head\\nattention runs $h$ attention operations in parallel on linear projections\\nof $Q, K, V$ into smaller subspaces, then concatenates and projects back:</p>\\n\\n<div class=\\"eq\\">\\n$\\\\mathrm{MHA}(X) = \\\\big[\\\\mathrm{head}_1; \\\\dots; \\\\mathrm{head}_h\\\\big] W^{O},\\n\\\\quad \\\\mathrm{head}_i = \\\\mathrm{Attention}(XW_i^{Q}, XW_i^{K}, XW_i^{V})$\\n</div>\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <svg class=\\"viz\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 980 310\\" role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"Multi-head attention splits token states into parallel attention heads then merges them\\">\\n    <defs>\\n      <marker id=\\"mha-flow-arrow\\" markerWidth=\\"10\\" markerHeight=\\"10\\" refX=\\"8\\" refY=\\"3\\" orient=\\"auto\\">\\n        <path d=\\"M0,0 L8,3 L0,6 Z\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\"></path>\\n      </marker>\\n    </defs>\\n    <text x=\\"30\\" y=\\"34\\" class=\\"title\\">Multi-head attention: several relevance functions in parallel</text>\\n    <text x=\\"30\\" y=\\"60\\" class=\\"muted\\">Each head sees a projected subspace, attends with its own pattern, and the model learns how to recombine them.</text>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"58\\" y=\\"132\\" width=\\"142\\" height=\\"74\\" rx=\\"14\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"129\\" y=\\"162\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">token states</text>\\n    <text x=\\"129\\" y=\\"185\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">X</text>\\n\\n    <g transform=\\"translate(302,92)\\">\\n      <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"176\\" height=\\"48\\" rx=\\"11\\" fill=\\"#f5f0ff\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\"></rect>\\n      <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"72\\" width=\\"176\\" height=\\"48\\" rx=\\"11\\" fill=\\"#f3f8f1\\" stroke=\\"#1a7f37\\"></rect>\\n      <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"144\\" width=\\"176\\" height=\\"48\\" rx=\\"11\\" fill=\\"#fff8e6\\" stroke=\\"#bf8700\\"></rect>\\n      <text x=\\"88\\" y=\\"30\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">head 1: syntax</text>\\n      <text x=\\"88\\" y=\\"102\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">head 2: reference</text>\\n      <text x=\\"88\\" y=\\"174\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">head 3: topic</text>\\n    </g>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"590\\" y=\\"116\\" width=\\"130\\" height=\\"104\\" rx=\\"14\\" fill=\\"#f6f8fa\\" stroke=\\"#d0d7de\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"655\\" y=\\"155\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">concat</text>\\n    <text x=\\"655\\" y=\\"181\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">heads</text>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"812\\" y=\\"132\\" width=\\"116\\" height=\\"74\\" rx=\\"14\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"870\\" y=\\"162\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">project</text>\\n    <text x=\\"870\\" y=\\"185\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">W^O</text>\\n\\n    <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M200 169 L302 116 M200 169 L302 188 M200 169 L302 260\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\" stroke-width=\\"2.1\\" marker-end=\\"url(#mha-flow-arrow)\\"></path>\\n    <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M478 116 L590 152 M478 188 L590 168 M478 260 L590 186\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\" stroke-width=\\"2.1\\" marker-end=\\"url(#mha-flow-arrow)\\"></path>\\n    <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M720 168 L812 168\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\" stroke-width=\\"2.5\\" marker-end=\\"url(#mha-flow-arrow)\\"></path>\\n    <text x=\\"498\\" y=\\"284\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">The labels are mental models: heads are not hand-assigned, but specialization often emerges.</text>\\n  </svg>\\n  <figcaption>\\n    Multi-head attention gives the model several parallel relevance functions before merging them back into one token representation.\\n  </figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n<pre><code class=\\"language-python\\">import torch, torch.nn as nn\\n\\nclass MultiHeadAttention(nn.Module):\\n    def __init__(self, d_model, n_heads):\\n        super().__init__()\\n        assert d_model % n_heads == 0\\n        self.h, self.d_k = n_heads, d_model // n_heads\\n        self.W_q = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model, bias=False)\\n        self.W_k = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model, bias=False)\\n        self.W_v = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model, bias=False)\\n        self.W_o = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model, bias=False)\\n\\n    def forward(self, x, causal=True):\\n        B, N, _ = x.shape\\n        def split(t): return t.view(B, N, self.h, self.d_k).transpose(1, 2)\\n        Q, K, V = split(self.W_q(x)), split(self.W_k(x)), split(self.W_v(x))\\n        out = F.scaled_dot_product_attention(Q, K, V, is_causal=causal)\\n        out = out.transpose(1, 2).contiguous().view(B, N, self.h * self.d_k)\\n        return self.W_o(out)\\n</code></pre>\\n\\n<div class=\\"tr-tip\\">\\n  <strong>Use the built\u2011in</strong>\\n  Since PyTorch 2.0, <code>F.scaled_dot_product_attention</code> dispatches to\\n  fused kernels (FlashAttention\u20112, memory\u2011efficient attention) where\\n  available. Hand\u2011rolling the einsum version is good for understanding,\\n  not for production.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div class=\\"tr-warn\\">\\n  <strong>Masking nuance</strong>\\n  The compact class above demonstrates decoder-only causal attention. Real\\n  training code also handles padding masks, packed sequences, and sometimes\\n  non-causal attention; pass those masks explicitly instead of assuming\\n  <code>is_causal=True</code> everywhere.\\n</div>\\n\\n<p>\\n  Multi-head attention can learn several kinds of relationships, but the operation still treats the input as a set unless\\n  we give it position. If the tokens <code>my name is</code> are shuffled, self-attention alone has no built-in reason to know\\n  the order changed. That is the next missing piece.\\n</p>\\n\\n<h2 id=\\"8-position-information-sinusoidal-vs-rope\\">8) Position information: sinusoidal vs RoPE</h2>\\n\\n<p>Self\u2011attention is <em>permutation\u2011equivariant</em> \u2014 shuffle the input tokens\\nand you get the same shuffled output. Language is not permutation\u2011invariant,\\nso we add positional information.</p>\\n\\n<h3 id=\\"sinusoidal-vaswani-et-al-original\\">Sinusoidal (Vaswani et al., original)</h3>\\n<p>Add a fixed sinusoidal vector to each token embedding before the first\\nlayer:</p>\\n<div class=\\"eq\\">\\n$\\\\mathrm{PE}(p, 2i) = \\\\sin\\\\!\\\\bigl(p / 10000^{2i/d}\\\\bigr),\\\\quad\\n \\\\mathrm{PE}(p, 2i+1) = \\\\cos\\\\!\\\\bigl(p / 10000^{2i/d}\\\\bigr)$\\n</div>\\n<p>Pros: simple. Cons: weak length extrapolation; baked into embeddings.</p>\\n\\n<h3 id=\\"rotary-position-embedding-rope-used-by-llama-mistral-qwen-gemma\\">Rotary Position Embedding \u2014 RoPE (used by LLaMA, Mistral, Qwen, Gemma)</h3>\\n<p>RoPE rotates pairs of dimensions of $Q$ and $K$ by an angle proportional\\nto the position. Because it acts on $Q$ and $K$ multiplicatively rather\\nthan additively on the embedding, the dot product\\n$Q_m^{\\\\top} K_n$ becomes a function of the <em>relative</em> position\\n$m - n$:</p>\\n<div class=\\"eq\\">\\n$\\\\langle\\\\mathrm{RoPE}(q, m),\\\\ \\\\mathrm{RoPE}(k, n)\\\\rangle\\n   = f(q, k, m - n)$\\n</div>\\n<p>This often improves long\u2011context behavior and is why many modern\\nopen\u2011weights LLMs use it. Variants like <strong>YaRN</strong> and\\n<strong>NTK\u2011aware scaling</strong> stretch RoPE\'s effective range to extend a\\n4k\u2011trained model to 32k+ tokens at inference.</p>\\n\\n<p>\\n  Once tokens can read each other and the model knows where those tokens sit, the rest of the Transformer block is engineering\\n  around stable deep learning: residual connections preserve signal, LayerNorm controls scale, and the feed-forward network\\n  transforms each token representation after attention has mixed context into it.\\n</p>\\n\\n<h2 id=\\"9-the-full-transformer-block\\">9) The full Transformer block</h2>\\n\\n<p>One block (decoder\u2011only flavor) is:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"language-python\\">class Block(nn.Module):\\n    def __init__(self, d_model, n_heads, d_ff):\\n        super().__init__()\\n        self.ln1 = nn.LayerNorm(d_model)\\n        self.attn = MultiHeadAttention(d_model, n_heads)\\n        self.ln2 = nn.LayerNorm(d_model)\\n        self.ff  = nn.Sequential(\\n            nn.Linear(d_model, d_ff), nn.GELU(),\\n            nn.Linear(d_ff, d_model),\\n        )\\n\\n    def forward(self, x):\\n        x = x + self.attn(self.ln1(x))   # pre\u2011norm residual\\n        x = x + self.ff(self.ln2(x))\\n        return x\\n</code></pre>\\n\\n<p>Stack $L$ blocks, prepend a token\u2011embedding + positional encoding, append\\na final LayerNorm and an unembedding (often tied to the input embedding),\\nand you have a GPT\u2011style language model.</p>\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <svg class=\\"viz\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 980 330\\" role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"Decoder-only Transformer block with pre-norm attention feed-forward and residual paths\\">\\n    <defs>\\n      <marker id=\\"decoder-block-arrow\\" markerWidth=\\"10\\" markerHeight=\\"10\\" refX=\\"8\\" refY=\\"3\\" orient=\\"auto\\">\\n        <path d=\\"M0,0 L8,3 L0,6 Z\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\"></path>\\n      </marker>\\n    </defs>\\n    <text x=\\"30\\" y=\\"34\\" class=\\"title\\">One modern decoder block</text>\\n    <text x=\\"30\\" y=\\"60\\" class=\\"muted\\">Pre-norm stabilizes deep stacks; residual paths preserve the token stream while attention and MLP update it.</text>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"74\\" y=\\"142\\" width=\\"106\\" height=\\"54\\" rx=\\"11\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"127\\" y=\\"175\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">input x</text>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"246\\" y=\\"128\\" width=\\"118\\" height=\\"44\\" rx=\\"10\\" fill=\\"#f6f8fa\\" stroke=\\"#d0d7de\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"305\\" y=\\"156\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">LayerNorm</text>\\n    <rect x=\\"410\\" y=\\"118\\" width=\\"156\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"13\\" fill=\\"#f5f0ff\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"488\\" y=\\"144\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">masked</text>\\n    <text x=\\"488\\" y=\\"166\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">self-attention</text>\\n    <circle cx=\\"632\\" cy=\\"150\\" r=\\"19\\" fill=\\"#fff8e6\\" stroke=\\"#bf8700\\"></circle>\\n    <text x=\\"632\\" y=\\"156\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">+</text>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"246\\" y=\\"232\\" width=\\"118\\" height=\\"44\\" rx=\\"10\\" fill=\\"#f6f8fa\\" stroke=\\"#d0d7de\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"305\\" y=\\"260\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">LayerNorm</text>\\n    <rect x=\\"410\\" y=\\"222\\" width=\\"156\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"13\\" fill=\\"#f3f8f1\\" stroke=\\"#1a7f37\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"488\\" y=\\"248\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">feed-forward</text>\\n    <text x=\\"488\\" y=\\"270\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">MLP</text>\\n    <circle cx=\\"632\\" cy=\\"254\\" r=\\"19\\" fill=\\"#fff8e6\\" stroke=\\"#bf8700\\"></circle>\\n    <text x=\\"632\\" y=\\"260\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">+</text>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"744\\" y=\\"194\\" width=\\"126\\" height=\\"58\\" rx=\\"12\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"807\\" y=\\"228\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">output</text>\\n\\n    <path d=\\"M180 169 L246 150 M364 150 L410 150 M566 150 L613 150 M651 150 L704 150 L704 223 L744 223\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\" stroke-width=\\"2.3\\" fill=\\"none\\" marker-end=\\"url(#decoder-block-arrow)\\"></path>\\n    <path d=\\"M180 169 C320 80 558 82 632 131\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\" stroke-width=\\"2.2\\" fill=\\"none\\" marker-end=\\"url(#decoder-block-arrow)\\"></path>\\n    <path d=\\"M364 254 L410 254 M566 254 L613 254 M651 254 L744 235\\" stroke=\\"#1a7f37\\" stroke-width=\\"2.3\\" fill=\\"none\\" marker-end=\\"url(#decoder-block-arrow)\\"></path>\\n    <path d=\\"M632 169 C632 194 632 210 632 235\\" stroke=\\"#bf8700\\" stroke-width=\\"2.2\\" fill=\\"none\\" marker-end=\\"url(#decoder-block-arrow)\\"></path>\\n    <path d=\\"M704 223 C510 318 300 318 180 196\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\" stroke-width=\\"2.2\\" fill=\\"none\\" marker-end=\\"url(#decoder-block-arrow)\\"></path>\\n    <text x=\\"490\\" y=\\"306\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">Each block keeps token shape n x d_model, so many blocks can be stacked.</text>\\n  </svg>\\n  <figcaption>\\n    A modern decoder block is pre-norm plus residual attention, followed by pre-norm plus residual feed-forward transformation.\\n  </figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n<p>\\n  The same block can be arranged into three families depending on what information is allowed to flow. If the whole input is\\n  known, use bidirectional encoder attention. If the model must generate left-to-right, use causal decoder attention. If the\\n  task has a separate source and target sequence, combine a bidirectional encoder, a causal decoder, and cross-attention.\\n</p>\\n\\n<h2 id=\\"10-encoder-decoder-encoder-decoder\\">10) Encoder, decoder, encoder-decoder</h2>\\n<table>\\n  <tbody><tr><th>Variant</th><th>Attention pattern</th><th>Examples</th><th>Used for</th></tr>\\n  <tr>\\n    <td>Encoder\u2011only</td><td>Bidirectional (no mask)</td>\\n    <td>BERT, RoBERTa, DeBERTa, modern embedding models</td>\\n    <td>Classification, embeddings, retrieval</td>\\n  </tr>\\n  <tr>\\n    <td>Decoder\u2011only</td><td>Causal mask</td>\\n    <td>GPT\u2011x, LLaMA, Mistral, Qwen, Gemma, Claude, DeepSeek</td>\\n    <td>Generation, chat, code, agents</td>\\n  </tr>\\n  <tr>\\n    <td>Encoder\u2011decoder</td><td>Bidirectional encoder + causal decoder + cross\u2011attention</td>\\n    <td>T5, FLAN\u2011T5, BART, original NMT Transformer</td>\\n    <td>Translation, summarization, seq\u2011to\u2011seq</td>\\n  </tr>\\n</tbody></table>\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <svg class=\\"viz\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 980 315\\" role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"Encoder-only decoder-only and encoder-decoder Transformer family variants\\">\\n    <defs>\\n      <marker id=\\"family-flow-arrow\\" markerWidth=\\"10\\" markerHeight=\\"10\\" refX=\\"8\\" refY=\\"3\\" orient=\\"auto\\">\\n        <path d=\\"M0,0 L8,3 L0,6 Z\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\"></path>\\n      </marker>\\n    </defs>\\n    <text x=\\"30\\" y=\\"34\\" class=\\"title\\">Transformer families are attention-permission patterns</text>\\n    <text x=\\"30\\" y=\\"60\\" class=\\"muted\\">The core block stays recognizable; masking and cross-attention determine what information can flow.</text>\\n\\n    <g transform=\\"translate(54,108)\\">\\n      <text x=\\"126\\" y=\\"-16\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">Encoder-only</text>\\n      <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"24\\" width=\\"252\\" height=\\"118\\" rx=\\"16\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n      <circle cx=\\"62\\" cy=\\"82\\" r=\\"22\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></circle>\\n      <circle cx=\\"126\\" cy=\\"82\\" r=\\"22\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></circle>\\n      <circle cx=\\"190\\" cy=\\"82\\" r=\\"22\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></circle>\\n      <path d=\\"M62 60 C90 30 158 30 190 60 M190 104 C156 136 94 136 62 104\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\" stroke-width=\\"2\\" fill=\\"none\\"></path>\\n      <text x=\\"126\\" y=\\"164\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">bidirectional understanding</text>\\n    </g>\\n\\n    <g transform=\\"translate(364,108)\\">\\n      <text x=\\"126\\" y=\\"-16\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">Decoder-only</text>\\n      <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"24\\" width=\\"252\\" height=\\"118\\" rx=\\"16\\" fill=\\"#f5f0ff\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\"></rect>\\n      <g transform=\\"translate(62,50)\\">\\n        <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#8250df\\" opacity=\\".9\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"40\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#f6f8fa\\" stroke=\\"#d0d7de\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"80\\" y=\\"0\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#f6f8fa\\" stroke=\\"#d0d7de\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"40\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#8250df\\" opacity=\\".45\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"40\\" y=\\"40\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#8250df\\" opacity=\\".9\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"80\\" y=\\"40\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#f6f8fa\\" stroke=\\"#d0d7de\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"80\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#8250df\\" opacity=\\".25\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"40\\" y=\\"80\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#8250df\\" opacity=\\".45\\"></rect>\\n        <rect x=\\"80\\" y=\\"80\\" width=\\"34\\" height=\\"34\\" fill=\\"#8250df\\" opacity=\\".9\\"></rect>\\n      </g>\\n      <text x=\\"126\\" y=\\"164\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">left-to-right generation</text>\\n    </g>\\n\\n    <g transform=\\"translate(674,108)\\">\\n      <text x=\\"126\\" y=\\"-16\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">Encoder-decoder</text>\\n      <rect x=\\"0\\" y=\\"34\\" width=\\"104\\" height=\\"58\\" rx=\\"12\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n      <rect x=\\"148\\" y=\\"34\\" width=\\"104\\" height=\\"58\\" rx=\\"12\\" fill=\\"#f5f0ff\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\"></rect>\\n      <rect x=\\"54\\" y=\\"126\\" width=\\"144\\" height=\\"44\\" rx=\\"11\\" fill=\\"#f3f8f1\\" stroke=\\"#1a7f37\\"></rect>\\n      <text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"68\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">encoder</text>\\n      <text x=\\"200\\" y=\\"68\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">decoder</text>\\n      <text x=\\"126\\" y=\\"153\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">cross-attention</text>\\n      <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M104 63 L148 63\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\" stroke-width=\\"2.2\\" marker-end=\\"url(#family-flow-arrow)\\"></path>\\n      <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M52 92 L96 126 M200 92 L156 126\\" stroke=\\"#1a7f37\\" stroke-width=\\"2.2\\" marker-end=\\"url(#family-flow-arrow)\\"></path>\\n    </g>\\n  </svg>\\n  <figcaption>\\n    The main Transformer families differ by masking and where information is allowed to flow, not by a completely different core operation.\\n  </figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n<h2 id=\\"11-inference-kv-cache\\">11) Inference: KV cache</h2>\\n\\n<p>At training time you process the whole sequence in parallel. At\\ngeneration time you produce one token at a time. Naively you would\\nrecompute all of $K$ and $V$ for every prefix \u2014 quadratic per token.\\nInstead you cache the per\u2011layer $K$ and $V$ tensors:</p>\\n<ul>\\n  <li>For token $t$, compute only the new $q_t, k_t, v_t$.</li>\\n  <li>Append $k_t, v_t$ to the cache.</li>\\n  <li>Attention is now $q_t$ against the cached $K, V$ \u2014 linear in $t$.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <svg class=\\"viz\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 980 300\\" role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"KV cache prefill and decode flow for Transformer inference\\">\\n    <defs>\\n      <marker id=\\"kv-cache-arrow\\" markerWidth=\\"10\\" markerHeight=\\"10\\" refX=\\"8\\" refY=\\"3\\" orient=\\"auto\\">\\n        <path d=\\"M0,0 L8,3 L0,6 Z\\" fill=\\"#0969da\\"></path>\\n      </marker>\\n    </defs>\\n    <text x=\\"30\\" y=\\"34\\" class=\\"title\\">KV cache: keep old keys and values instead of recomputing them</text>\\n    <text x=\\"30\\" y=\\"60\\" class=\\"muted\\">Prefill builds the cache for the prompt; each decode step appends only the new token\'s key and value.</text>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"58\\" y=\\"124\\" width=\\"154\\" height=\\"58\\" rx=\\"12\\" fill=\\"#eaf3ff\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"135\\" y=\\"148\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">prompt prefill</text>\\n    <text x=\\"135\\" y=\\"170\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">all prompt tokens</text>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"292\\" y=\\"98\\" width=\\"232\\" height=\\"112\\" rx=\\"16\\" fill=\\"#f5f0ff\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"408\\" y=\\"126\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">per-layer cache</text>\\n    <rect x=\\"326\\" y=\\"148\\" width=\\"48\\" height=\\"36\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\"></rect>\\n    <rect x=\\"386\\" y=\\"148\\" width=\\"48\\" height=\\"36\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\"></rect>\\n    <rect x=\\"446\\" y=\\"148\\" width=\\"48\\" height=\\"36\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"350\\" y=\\"171\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">K,V</text>\\n    <text x=\\"410\\" y=\\"171\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">K,V</text>\\n    <text x=\\"470\\" y=\\"171\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">K,V</text>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"612\\" y=\\"124\\" width=\\"146\\" height=\\"58\\" rx=\\"12\\" fill=\\"#f3f8f1\\" stroke=\\"#1a7f37\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"685\\" y=\\"148\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">decode step</text>\\n    <text x=\\"685\\" y=\\"170\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">new q,k,v</text>\\n\\n    <rect x=\\"820\\" y=\\"124\\" width=\\"112\\" height=\\"58\\" rx=\\"12\\" fill=\\"#fff8e6\\" stroke=\\"#bf8700\\"></rect>\\n    <text x=\\"876\\" y=\\"148\\" class=\\"label\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">next token</text>\\n    <text x=\\"876\\" y=\\"170\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">append</text>\\n\\n    <path fill=\\"none\\" d=\\"M212 153 L292 153 M524 153 L612 153 M758 153 L820 153\\" stroke=\\"#0969da\\" stroke-width=\\"2.4\\" marker-end=\\"url(#kv-cache-arrow)\\"></path>\\n    <path d=\\"M876 182 C876 246 346 246 346 184\\" stroke=\\"#8250df\\" stroke-width=\\"2.5\\" fill=\\"none\\" marker-end=\\"url(#kv-cache-arrow)\\"></path>\\n    <text x=\\"496\\" y=\\"264\\" class=\\"muted\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">The next step queries all cached K,V, but only computes projections for the new token.</text>\\n  </svg>\\n  <figcaption>\\n    KV caching preserves the same autoregressive loop while avoiding recomputation of old keys and values at every decode step.\\n  </figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n<p>During decode, the KV cache is a major reason LLM inference often becomes\\nmemory-bandwidth-bound: every new token must read the cached keys and values.\\nPrefill over a long prompt can still be compute-heavy. The cache is also why\\ncontext length costs RAM (each extra token adds roughly\\n<code>2 \xb7 n_layers \xb7 n_kv_heads \xb7 d_head \xb7 dtype_bytes</code> per request; with\\nMQA/GQA, <code>n_kv_heads</code> can be smaller than the number of query heads).</p>\\n\\n<pre><code class=\\"language-python\\"># Cached decode sketch.\\n# Prefill computes K/V for the prompt once. Each later step only computes\\n# the new token\'s q/k/v and attends q_new against cached K/V.\\n\\ntokens = tokenize(\\"my name is\\")\\nlogits, cache = gpt_prefill(tokens)          # cache stores per-layer K/V for the prompt\\nnext_token = sample(logits[-1])\\n\\nwhile next_token != EOS_ID:\\n    tokens.append(next_token)\\n    logits, cache = gpt_decode_step([next_token], cache=cache)\\n    next_token = sample(logits[-1])</code></pre>\\n\\n<p>\\n  KV caching fixes wasted recomputation during generation, but it does not make attention free. Long context still means\\n  more cached keys and values to read, and full attention over long prompts still has heavy memory traffic. That pressure\\n  is why modern LLM systems use optimized attention kernels and smaller key/value layouts.\\n</p>\\n\\n<h2 id=\\"12-efficiency-flashattention-mqa-gqa-sliding-window\\">12) Efficiency: FlashAttention, MQA, GQA, sliding window</h2>\\n<ul>\\n  <li><strong>FlashAttention</strong> (Dao et al., 2022; v2 2023; v3 2024) computes the\\n      same softmax attention but tiles the work to keep $QK^{\\\\top}$ in\\n      on\u2011chip SRAM and never materializes the full $n \\\\times n$ matrix in\\n      HBM. Result: ~2\u20134\xd7 speedup and much less memory.</li>\\n  <li><strong>Multi\u2011Query Attention (MQA)</strong> shares one $K$/$V$ projection across\\n      all heads \u2014 shrinks the KV cache by $h\\\\times$. Used in PaLM.</li>\\n  <li><strong>Grouped\u2011Query Attention (GQA)</strong> is the compromise: one $K$/$V$\\n      per group of $g$ query heads. Used in LLaMA\u20112/3, Mistral, Qwen2,\\n      Gemma 2 \u2014 a common modern choice for decoder-only LLMs.</li>\\n  <li><strong>Sliding\u2011window attention</strong> (Mistral, Gemma) restricts each token\\n      to attend only to the last $W$ tokens, lowering the attention cost\\n      from $O(n^2)$ to $O(nW)$.</li>\\n  <li><strong>Mixture of Experts (MoE)</strong> (Mixtral, DeepSeek\u2011V3) replaces the\\n      feed\u2011forward block with a router that picks $k$ of $E$ experts per\\n      token \u2014 more parameters, similar FLOPs.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h2 id=\\"13-pulling-it-all-together\\">13) Pulling it all together</h2>\\n<ol>\\n  <li>Embed tokens; add positional info (sinusoidal, learned, or RoPE).</li>\\n  <li>Stack $L$ Transformer blocks (pre\u2011norm + residual + GQA + SwiGLU/GELU FFN).</li>\\n  <li>Final LayerNorm + tied unembedding \u2192 logits over the vocabulary.</li>\\n  <li>For generation: KV cache + sampling (greedy / temperature / top\u2011p / top\u2011k).</li>\\n</ol>\\n\\n<div class=\\"tr-tip\\">\\n  <strong>What to read next</strong>\\n  Chapter 12 \u2014 LLM fine\u2011tuning \u2014 picks up exactly here, taking a pretrained\\n  decoder\u2011only Transformer and adapting it with PEFT (LoRA / QLoRA), then\\n  Chapter 13 covers preference alignment beyond DPO.\\n</div>\\n\\n</div>"}'),d={title:"Transformers & Attention \u2014 From RNN Memory to GPT Generation",slug:"/part5-deep-learning-and-llms/11-transformers-and-attention",description:"Read this chapter as one continuous design path. We are not introducing attention as a separate trick. We are answering the question left open by GRU/LSTM: if a decoder needs a specific old token, why force that infor...",sidebar_label:"11. Transformers and attention",pagination_label:"11. Transformers and attention",hide_title:!0,hide_table_of_contents:!0},l=void 0,c={},h=[];function f(e){return(0,o.jsx)(i.A,{content:s})}function m(e={}){const{wrapper:t}={...(0,r.R)(),...e.components};return t?(0,o.jsx)(t,{...e,children:(0,o.jsx)(f,{...e})}):f()}}}]);